# Threadneedle: Lares (Tasked Spirit)
## OCIF, round-trip fidelity, and Orion Reed's models — measured 2026-09-02

**Method note.** I read the spec from source (`git clone ocwg/spec` → now `ocwg/ocif-spec`, HEAD `8a980dcc`, 2026-04-10), ran the official `schema.json` against fabricated tiddler-bearing documents with Python `jsonschema` 4.26, cloned and `npm install`ed and *executed* the most active OCIF implementation, and resolved the spec's own schema URIs over the wire with `curl`. Where the field has not settled I say so. Mu lens: `*` Wand, `?` Cup, `!` Sword, `_` Pentacle, `0` unsettled.

---

## 1. `?` The OCIF data model as specified

Spec: `https://github.com/ocwg/ocif-spec/blob/main/spec/v0.7.0/spec.md` — `https://spec.canvasprotocol.org` 302s here. **OCWG Candidate Recommendation, March 2026**, CC BY-SA 4.0. Editor Dr. Max Völkel (ITMV / GraphInOut); authors alphabetically Aaron Franke (Godot Engine), Maikel van de Lisdonk (Code Flow Canvas), Jess Martin (sociotechnica), Orion Reed. `VERSION` file = `v0.7.0`. A `spec/v0.7.1-draft/` directory and a `v0.7.1-draft` branch sit in-tree, prepared 2026-03-24, never released.

### 1a. File structure — §File Structure

Six top-level keys; only `ocif` is required.

| Property | JSON Type | OCIF Type | Required | Contents |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `ocif` | `string` | URI | **required** | The URI of the OCIF schema |
| `rootNode` | `string` | ID | optional | A canvas root node |
| `data` | `array` | Extension[] | optional | Extended canvas data |
| `nodes` | `array` | Node[] | optional | A list of nodes |
| `resources` | `array` | Resource[] | optional | A list of resources |
| `schemas` | `array` | SchemaEntry[] | optional | Declared schemas |

The minimal valid OCIF file is literally `{"ocif": "https://canvasprotocol.org/ocif/v0.7.0"}`. `rootNode` "MUST point to a node defined within the `nodes` array."

### 1b. The type hierarchy — §Entity, §Item

New in v0.7.0. **Entity** = `{data, comment}`. **Item** = Entity + `id`. Node, Resource and the document itself are Item sub-types; Representation is an Entity sub-type. This is what lets extension data ride on *four* different carriers.

On `comment`, note the unusually strict language — one of only thirteen MUSTs in the document:

> "Comments exist to annotate OCIF files with additional information when reading the raw text of the file manually, like a comment in a programming language. **They MUST NOT be used for any functional purpose by software that processes OCIF files.** Comments MAY be preserved or discarded when processing OCIF files, and in either case the file is functionally equivalent."

`!` That closes off `comment` as a smuggling channel for us. Do not put lar URIs there.

### 1c. Nodes — §Nodes

> "A node is visible, also called visual node in this spec, if it has a position and size… A node without `position` and `size` can serve, for example, as a logical grouping or a purely structural edge."

**Structural** (§Structural Properties): `id` (**required**), `parent` (ID of a node; "If empty, the root node of the canvas is the parent node"), `deleteWithParent` (boolean, default `true`).

**Layout** (§Layout Properties): `position` (number[], default `[0,0]`), `size` (default `[100,100]`), `rotation` (Angle, default `0`), `rotationAxis` (number[3], default `[0,0,1]`), `scale` (number or Vector, default `1`).

The v0.7.0 change that matters most:

> "OCIF allows customizing the local coordinate system of a node relative to the parent coordinate system. **Every node creates its own local coordinate system**, in which displayed resources and child-nodes are interpreted… If a parent node is stated and changed, local coordinates need to be recalculated, taking the whole chain of nested coordinate systems into account. **This is a concept commonly found in game engines and infinitely zoomable canvases.** Mathematically, these are known as local-to-global transformations. Transforms are chainable."

Composition order is stated: "The full transformation matrix is 'TRS' ordered: `M = T * R * S`. So first scale (S, `scale`) is applied, then rotation (R, `rotation`, `rotationAxis`). OCIF has no translation (T)."

And a practical warning aimed straight at infinite canvas:

> "For interactive apps, the transforms allow to adapt on parent changes. Furthermore, **when zooming very large maps, position and size should be computed on the fly, as global positions would become unstable due to numeric precision.**"

**Content** (§Content Properties): `resource` (ID) and `resourceFit` (enum `none|containX|containY|contain|cover|fill|tile`, default `contain`, borrowed from CSS `object-fit`). The node rectangle "acts as a placement box and **clipping mask** on the contents of the node… In CSS, this is called `overflow: hidden`." Border z-order is specified: "The stroke SHOULD be rendered 'on top' of a resource, while the fill SHOULD be rendered 'behind' the resource."

**Extension** (§Extension Properties): `data` (Extension[]) and `comment`.

`*` **There is no text node and no image node.** §"Text Nodes": "There is no special text node in OCIF. Text is a kind of resource. A node can display a resource." §"Image Nodes": "There is no special image node in OCIF. An image is another kind of resource, which can be displayed by any node." **This is load-bearing for §3 below** — the spec's entire text story runs through `resources`.

### 1d. Relations — merged away in v0.7.0

§Changes, "From v0.6 to v0.7.0", first bullet: "**Merged node and relation.**" There is no longer a top-level `relations` array in the prose. Everything relational is now a node extension:

- **`@ocif/edge`** — `start` (ID, required), `end` (ID, required), `directed` (boolean, default `true`), `rel` (string). The `rel` field is explicitly semantic: "This field allows representing an **RDF triple (subject, predicate, object) as (start, rel, end)**." Guidance: "A graph with multiple edges should be expressed as multiple OCIF nodes, each having one edge extension. Using multiple (hyper-)edge extensions on the same node is discouraged."
- **`@ocif/group`** — `members` (ID[], required), `cascadeDelete` (boolean). "Groups can contain groups as members. Thus, all semantics apply recursively." Ungrouping deletes the group, not the members.
- **`@ocif/hyperedge`** — `endpoints` (required, **ordered** list), `weight` (default `1.0`), `rel`. Each endpoint is `{id, direction, weight}` with `direction ∈ {in, out, undir}`, default `undir`. "Such a model allows representing all kinds of hyper-edges, even rather obscure ones" — including "An edge with only one endpoint / An edge with no endpoints / An edge with only incoming or only outgoing endpoints."
- **`@ocif/inherit`** — `inheritFrom` (ID), `include` (string[]), `exclude` (string[]). "Inheritance MAY NOT form cycles." Lookup semantics: "When looking for JSON properties of a child and finding them undefined, an app should look for the same value in the parent… The chain of sources should be followed until a root is reached or a cycle is detected."
- **`@ocif/page`** — `pageNumber` (default `1`), `label`.

`!` Note the drift this created: `schema.json` **still carries a stale top-level `relations` property and a `$defs/relation`** ("A relation between nodes", with a `node` property pointing at "ID of a visual node, which represents this relation"). The prose merged relations away; the normative-ish JSON schema did not. I confirmed this by loading `spec/v0.7.0/schema.json` and enumerating `$defs`: `['entity','item','node','relation','resource','representation','schema']`.

### 1e. Assets — §Assets, §Resources, §Representation

Three storage modes: **Inline** (in the file, referenced by id), **External** (separate file, relative URI), **Remote** (absolute URI).

A Resource is `{id (required), representations (required), data, comment}`. Ordering is semantic:

> "The order of representations is significant. **The first representation is the default representation. Later representations can be used as fallbacks.**"

A Representation is `{location, mimeType, content, data, comment}` with "Either `content` or `location` MUST be present. If `content` is used, `location` must be left out and vice versa." The valid combinations are tabulated (inline text, inline binary/base64, remote, external, remote data-URI).

§Fallback gives the worked example that matters to us — SVG inline, then remote PNG, then `text/plain` — with the rationale: "If SVG content cannot be rendered by the application, the PNG can be used. The third representation is a text representation… **This can be used for accessibility or indexing purposes.**"

And in §Layout Properties, the spec states the correct degradation behaviour explicitly:

> "In general, a size property is useful as a **fallback to display a placeholder rectangle if the resource cannot be displayed as intended.**"

`!` **Hold onto that sentence. It is the one that makes §3 an implementation bug rather than a spec ambiguity.**

### 1f. Ports and anchors — §Ports Extension, §Anchored Node Extension

**Ports are nodes**, which is an unusual and rather good decision:

> "Any node can act as a port. The 'container' node uses the Ports Extension to define which nodes it uses as ports."

`@ocif/ports` = `{ports: ID[]}`. Constraint: "Each node SHOULD appear only in **one** ports array. A port cannot be used by multiple nodes as a port." In the spec's example, `p1` and `p2` are ordinary nodes with positions; `n1` merely *declares* them as its ports; an arrow then starts at `p1`.

`@ocif/anchored-node` = `{topLeftAnchor, bottomRightAnchor, topLeftOffset, bottomRightOffset}`, percentage coordinates in `[0,1]×[0,1]` relative to the parent's *effective* coordinate system: "The parent size is added to the position and yields the coordinate of the *one* unit." Defaults `[0,0]` and `[1,1]`. The spec distinguishes it from `parent`: "This extension is mainly useful to split the space of one node into several auto-resized areas. For placing elements like in a vector-drawing application, but relative to the parent node, the `parent` property is often a better tool."

`@ocif/global-positions` = `{globalPosition, globalSize, globalRotation}`, and it is explicitly a **cache**:

> "To make it easy for simple viewers to show OCIF content, an OCIF file may pre-compute global positions… **They are redundant**, as the core OCIF node properties fully define the global position… If the global positions disagree with the computed positions… the computed positions should be used… If a conflict is detected on the initial loading, a warning SHOULD be issued, and **the local position MUST be preferred.**"

`*` That is a well-designed slot for exactly what DreamDOM will compute anyway.

### 1g. Style — §Style Extensions

`@ocif/textstyle` = `{fontSizePx (12), fontFamily ("sans-serif"), color (#000000), align (left|right|center|justify), bold, italic}`. Deliberately thin: "It is much simpler than CSS… OCIF supports no other units."

`@ocif/theme-define` and `@ocif/theme-use` are more interesting than they look. A theme-define node holds named branches, each containing a nested `data` array of extensions:

```json
{"type":"@ocif/theme-define",
 "dark":  {"data":[{"type":"@ocif/textstyle","color":"#FFFFFF"}]},
 "light": {"data":[{"type":"@ocif/textstyle","color":"#000000"}]}}
```

> "So the theme **branches a node content into several possible worlds** and defines any values, including those in extensions."

`@ocif/theme-use` = `{selectTheme}`, inherits downward through the parent-child tree, and `"selectTheme": null` explicitly re-selects the default further down. `_` Possible-worlds branching over arbitrary extension data is a general mechanism, not a colour feature — worth noting for anything we want to vary by face or plane.

### 1h. Root node and nested canvases — §Root Node, §Nesting Canvases

> "Every canvas has **one** defined or implied root node. The root node itself SHOULD not be rendered, only its interior. If no root node is defined, an implied root with ID `rootNode` is used… The root node cannot be used as a child of another node within the same file, **but instances may be child nodes in another file.**"

`size` on the root acts as an SVG-like viewbox. `position` and `rotation` "MUST NOT be set. If the root node has either of those properties set, the app should ignore their values and emit a warning."

Nesting is where OCIF becomes composable:

> "A node 'A' in a canvas (now called 'host canvas') may use a resource of type `application/ocif+json`… That resource defines another canvas (now called 'sub-canvas')… From the perspective of the host canvas, **node 'A' and the sub-canvas root node 'B' are the same node.** The properties of the host canvas node 'A' extend and overwrite the properties of the imported child canvas root node. **This even overwrites the `id` property**… That is, the host node 'A' is interpreted as a **JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396)** document against the sub-canvas root node 'B'. **There is one exception: `data`-extension arrays are always merged**… Both arrays are appended, first the sub-canvas root node's extensions, then the host node 'A' extensions. **Later entries overwrite earlier entries for the same `type` of extension.**"

§Partial Export specifies the inverse operation: exporting a node and its subtree makes it the new root, all inherited effective values are copied onto it, and "`position`, `rotation`, `rotationAxis`, and `parent` MUST be removed from the new root node, as the root defines the origin (0,0) of the new canvas."

`*` **This is a lararium-in-a-lararium primitive, already specified, with a stated merge semantics.** It is better written down than anything we currently have for the same operation.

### 1i. `*` Transclusion — §Nodes as Resources

This section is the one that made me take OCIF seriously, and I quote it at length because it is the crux of question 9:

> "**Motivation: Using a node B as a resource in another node A can be seen as a form of _transclusion_.** In HTML, an `IFRAME` on page A can show a page B. Similarly, by using another node as a resource, the importing node establishes another view (in CSS terms, a _view port_) on the canvas. This allows a node B to be seen in multiple places on the canvas: Once at the original location of B and additionally in n other places where n other nodes use node B as a resource.
>
> **Every node in an OCIF document is automatically available as a resource, too. They are addressed by prefixing the node id with `#`.** Nodes need not be added to the `resources` array."

The implicit resource has `mimeType: "application/ocif-node+json"` and the node's own JSON as `content`. Semantics:

> "Node A establishes a kind of 'viewport' onto node B… node B is rendered in isolation, only taking all of its (transitive) children into account… **Different from normal resources, here the intention is to create a live view (not a static image) into the canvas. Whenever the way B looks is changed, the other places where node B is imported should be updated, too.**
>
> **Transclusions may not form 'loops', that is, a node MAY NOT directly or indirectly import itself. If such a loop is present, all stated imports of the loop MUST be ignored and a warning SHOULD be given.**"

`!` Live transclusion with cycle refusal, specified. But mark the difference precisely, because question 9 turns on it: **OCIF transclusion is node→node, whole-node, and geometric.** It is an iframe. Ours is `{{title}}` at arbitrary depth *inside wikitext*, mid-paragraph, filtered, templated, recursive through macros. OCIF can carry the *fact* "A transcludes B" as an edge or a nodes-as-resource reference. It has no representation for *position within the text*.

### 1j. Schemas — §Schemas

A schema is JSON Schema 2020-12. Entries are `{uri (required), schema, location, name}`. The `name` is an alias: "MUST start with a `@` character. Names SHOULD use the convention organisation name `/` type (`node` or `rel`) `/` schema name… Names MUST be unique within an OCIF file." Version convention: "By convention, schema names do not contain a version number. However, if multiple versions of the same schema are used in a file, the version number MUST be appended."

Sub-type addressing uses JSON Pointer: `https://example.com/myschema.json#typename` → `/$defs/typename`.

Built-in mapping (§Built-in Schema Mappings): "Any Schema Name of the form `@ocif/{name}` maps to a schema URI `https://spec.canvasprotocol.org/v0.7.0/extensions/{name}.json`." The appendix materialises all seventeen and says "The following block can be assumed to be present in every OCIF document."

`_` And note the design-decision doc's candour about JSON Schema's limits: "Schema composition is tricky. Cannot define very pretty, compact things like namespace WHILE using schema composition. **Resolution: We use JSON Schema where it helps us, but are not constrained by its limitations.**"

### 1k. Primitive types — §OCIF Types

Relevant constraints only:

- **ID**: "It **MAY NOT start with a hash-mark (#)**"; no control characters; "should be valid HTML IDs and **if possible even valid CSS identifiers**" — quoting CSS2 syntax, i.e. `[a-zA-Z0-9]`, U+00A0 and higher, hyphen, underscore; cannot start with a digit. "It must be unique among all IDs used in an OCIF document. **The ID space is shared among nodes and resources.**"
- **URI**: RFC 3986. Unrestricted scheme — so `lar:` is a legal *extension type* URI.
- **Vector**: 2D or 3D; "In 2D, the z-axis coordinate SHOULD be used for relative z-index ordering"; scalar shortcut `3` → `[3,3,3]`.
- **Angle**: −360..360 degrees. `!` The prose is self-contradictory: §Layout Properties says rotation is "in degrees in **counter-clockwise** direction", while §Angle says "positive values (0,360] indicating a **clockwise** rotation." Unresolved in the CR.

`!` **Consequence for us:** `lar:///the-door.names.the-whole-arc` contains `:` and `/`, so it is a valid HTML id but **not** a valid CSS identifier — SHOULD-level friction. Combined with the fact that every implementation I ran rewrites ids anyway, the ruling is: **never use a lar URI as an OCIF node id.** Carry it inside the extension; use a stable opaque id outside.

### 1l. Practical recommendations

Proposed MIME type `application/ocif+json` — **not IANA-registered**; the spec carries an HTML comment pointing at `ocwg/spec` issue #13. File extension `.ocif.json` or `.ocif`. ID collision rule: "If IDs collide, the first defined ID should be used… A warning SHOULD be emitted." Schema hosting: "A schema MUST have a URI as its identifier. A schema SHOULD be hosted at its URI," with a Cool-URIs content-negotiation recommendation.

`!` The MIME type is unregistered *and* implementations disagree: `tldraw/ocif` exports `OCIF_FILE_MIMETYPE = 'application/vnd.ocif+json'` — `vnd.` prefixed, not the spec's string.

---

## 2. `!` The extension mechanism in normative detail — and the sentence that decides interop

### 2a. What an extension is — §Extension Mechanism

The framing first (§Extensions):

> "No two canvas applications are alike: There are apps for informal whiteboarding, formal diagramming, quick visual sketches, node-and-wire programming, and many other use cases. Each of these apps has radically different feature sets. **Extensions are an integral part of OCIF.** They allow adding custom data to **nodes**, **resources**, and the whole **canvas**."

The mechanism:

> "- An extension _is_ a JSON object (used as a '**property bag**') with one mandatory, reserved property: `type`. **The extension can use all other property keys.**
> - **Arbitrary, nested JSON structures are allowed.**
> - Extensions SHOULD define how the OCIF properties play together with the extension properties and ideally with other (known) extensions.
> - Elements (nodes, resources, canvas) can have multiple extensions within their `data` array."

| Property | JSON Type | OCIF Type | Required | Contents |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | `string` | Schema Name or URI | **required** | Type of extension |

> "**type**: The type of the extension. This is a URI or a simple name. **If a name is used, that name must be present in the schemas section, where it is mapped to a URI.**"

`!` **The same-type merge rule, which is a trap.** If one element carries two extensions with the same `type`, "the JSON fragments of the objects are by default considered to override each other, as defined in JSON Merge Pat[c]h RFC 7386." The spec's worked example merges `{fruit:apple, color:blue}` and `{color:red, city:Karlsruhe}` into `{fruit:apple, color:red, city:Karlsruhe}`. Rationale: "This makes combining files by hand easier and uses the same mechanism as the inheritance extension and nested canvases."

This makes the generic `@ocif/data` extension **actively unsafe for multi-app use** — its own schema pins `type` to the constant `"@ocif/data"`, so two different applications stashing state there on the same node silently clobber each other key-by-key. `_` **Ruling for us: mint our own URI-identified type. Never ride `@ocif/data`.**

### 2b. How a URI-identified extension is declared — §Defining Extensions

> "If you need to store some extra data at a node for your canvas app, and none of the existing extensions fit, you can define your own extension.
>
> **An extension MUST have a URI (as its ID) and a document describing the extension.**
>
> It SHOULD have a version number, as part of its URI. It SHOULD have a proposed name and SHOULD have a JSON schema.
>
> The proposed structure is to use a directory in a git repository… Within the repo, there SHOULD be two files: README.md, which describes the extension. schema.json, which contains the JSON schema… **This schema MUST use the same URI as the extension.**"

Step-by-step (§How To Write an Extension Step-by-Step): define the properties; define the URI, "Ideally, this is where you publish your JSON schema file"; write the semantics; create the JSON schema — "**Large language models are a great help here**" *(their words, in a Candidate Recommendation)*. Suggested layout: `/1.0/README.md` + `/1.0/schema.json`.

**No registration, no approval, no gatekeeper.** The cookbook is explicit: "Extensions can be defined and used **in the same OCIF document, without any prior announcement or registration process.**" `catalog.md` exists but "Getting listed in this file is **not** a requirement for an extension to be used… Listing in this file is not an endorsement."

`_` Worth recording: **`catalog.md` lists zero third-party extensions.** Every entry is OCWG's own, and every one is still at v0.5 URIs. In two and a half years nobody has registered an extension. We would be the first.

Export guidance (§Exporting Data with Extensions): "the application SHOULD use inline or external schemas for the extensions. Remote schemas CAN be used to save space."

### 2c. `!` The sentence that decides interop

**There are exactly three statements in the entire corpus about meeting an extension you do not understand, and they do not agree with each other.**

**(1) §Canvas Extensions — the only RFC-shaped MUST on the topic, and it says *ignore*:**

> "Like for nodes, canvas-level extensions are carried in an array named `data` on the root object of the OCIF file. Each entry in this array is an extension object with a `type` that identifies the extension and any extension-specific properties. Applications MAY add any number of canvas extensions; **unknown extensions MUST be ignored.**"

**(2) §Data Extension — preservation, lowercase, non-normative:**

> "A data extension is a generic extension to carry data that has no semantics within the OCIF format… Semantics:
> - **Like all extensions, apps should preserve unknown extensions and round-trip them on export.**"

**(3) §Handling Extension Data — the intent, in plain prose with no keyword at all:**

> "To support interchange between canvases when features don't overlap, **canvas apps need to preserve nodes that they don't support**:
> - Canvas A supporting Feature X creates a canvas with a Feature X node in it and exports it as OCIF.
> - Canvas B, which does not support Feature X, opens the OCIF file, and some edits are made to the canvas.
> - Canvas B exports the canvas to an OCIF file. **The nodes for Feature X should still be in the OCIF file, unchanged.**"

The strongest formulation anywhere lives in a *design* document, not the spec — `design/requirements.md` §Extensible:

> "We want a number of apps, including those not known at time of writing this spec, be able to use the export format to round-trip data. Thus,
> - an app may write arbitrary data into the export (given it adheres to some rules)
> - **an app may not alter or delete data written be another app** (unless the whole node is deleted)"

And `design/goals.md` §Round-Tripping: "An app A can export to OCIF and embed its native data structures within, so that any app B can work without, re-export, and allow app A to reconstruct its native data structures."

### 2d. `!` The finding that settles the normative question

I ran `grep -in "2119\|8174\|conformance\|normative\|key words" spec/v0.7.0/spec.md`. **It returns nothing.**

The document has **no RFC 2119 boilerplate, no RFC 8174 clause, no conformance section, and no conformance classes.** There is no defined notion of a "conforming OCIF processor," no distinction between producer and consumer conformance, and no statement of what makes a document invalid versus merely unusual. "MUST" appears thirteen times as ordinary editorial emphasis. `grep -c "MUST"` = 13; the complete list of MUST / MUST NOT / MAY NOT / SHOULD NOT lines runs to fourteen occurrences across the whole 2010-line document, and I read every one.

`0` **Therefore: preserve-and-passthrough is the stated intent of the working group and is not a requirement of the specification.** The only MUST that touches unknown extensions says *ignore*. Preservation is a lowercase "should" in a section about a convenience extension, plus a narrative walkthrough with no keyword, plus a bullet in a design doc that the spec does not incorporate by reference.

The spec's own editor knows the gap is real and unclosed. Discussion #63 (`github.com/orgs/ocwg/discussions/63`), xamde, 2025-08-07:

> "A particular case we did think about is a 'generic merge'. Use case: App A exports OCIF. App B reads it, **discards all elements it does not know (it should not do this, but some apps will do this.** Or another external converter converts OCIF to app-B-format, which has no extension semantics.). So then app B loads the data, user edits it, and stores back as app-b-native format… Now we have before.ocif.json (went into app B) and after.ocif.json (came out of app B). **Challenge: Compare both files, understand the diff… Then, apply the diff back to the before.ocif.json, so that the extension data of all nodes, which have not been deleted, are still there.** I guess, in this process lots of things can go wrong… **So in short, yes, such a linter is an excellent, missing piece :-)**"

And the group's chair conceded the same limit to Obsidian's Steph Ango (kepano) in Discussion #44, 2025-01-18:

> "your comment hits on a bigger issue: **the OCWG and the OCIF format can really only enable _interchange_. True interoperability will have to be a choice by each tool-maker.** … We ran into this problem many times as we worked through designing the spec. As a group, we called this '**preserving the property bag**'… Ultimately, if a tool-maker wants to support interoperability with other tools that have features that they don't currently support, **they have to commit to preserving the data** for the unsupported extensions and then re-exporting that to the OCIF files."

kepano's original question is worth having in the record because it is our question, asked eighteen months earlier by someone with a shipping product:

> "It seems like what we're learning is that **canvas data is hard to make interoperable because the fallbacks when functionality is missing from an app are ambiguous and less graceful than they are with something like Markdown.** The question I ask myself is if we were building Obsidian Canvas today and this format already existed, would we have used it? … **Perhaps there is room for OCWG to specify fallback behaviors for optional features.**"

`!` **Answer to your question 2, stated flatly: the spec REQUIRES ignore. It RECOMMENDS, in non-normative voice, preserve-and-passthrough. It never requires it, and it never permits refusal.** Refusing the file is not contemplated anywhere.

### 2e. The unenforced binding — measured

The prose says a short name "must be present in the schemas section." I tested whether anything checks. Document:

```json
{"ocif":"https://canvasprotocol.org/ocif/v0.7.0",
 "nodes":[{"id":"n1","position":[0,0],
   "data":[{"type":"@lar/tiddler","uri":"lar:///x"},
           {"type":"@ocif/data","a":1},{"type":"@ocif/data","b":2}]}]}
```

Validated against `spec/v0.7.0/schema.json`: **0 errors.** No `schemas` entry, an undeclared `@`-name, and two colliding `@ocif/data` blocks — all clean. The spec's own appendix example (`@example/circle`) omits the schemas entry too.

Cause: `schema.json` declares `data` as bare `{"type":"array"}` with **no `items`**, and there is no `additionalProperties: false` and no `unevaluatedProperties` anywhere in the file (`grep` confirms zero occurrences). Its only `required` is `["ocif"]`, and it does not even constrain the *value* of `ocif`. I confirmed the permissiveness with a deliberate garbage control:

```json
{"ocif":"x","nodes":[{"id":"a","data":[{"NO_TYPE":1}],"position":"not-an-array"}],
 "totallyMadeUpTopLevel":{"a":1}}
```

→ **one** error, and only the obvious one: `nodes[0].position 'not-an-array' is not of type 'array'`. A `data` entry with no `type` at all, an invented top-level key, and a bogus version string all pass.

---

## 3. `*` The measurement in full

### 3a. The instrument

`github.com/tldraw/ocif` — created 2026-02-06, **last commit 2026-08-10** (three weeks before measurement), MIT, author Mime Čuvalo. Its README claims exactly the behaviour in question:

> "**Interoperability** — Unknown node extensions are preserved on import (in `shape.meta`) and re-exported unchanged, **as required by the OCIF spec**"

Its own suite is green: `npx vitest run` → **91 tests passed** across `export.test.ts` (25), `ocif.test.ts` (48), `smoke.test.ts` (18).

I cloned it, ran `npm install` (exit 0), confirmed the baseline suite green, then added probe files under `src/__tests__/probe/` using **their own harness** — `importRecords()` / `roundTrip()` copied verbatim from `src/__tests__/export.test.ts:8-19` — so that nothing in my setup differed from the tests that pass.

*(Instrument-collision note, per standing practice: my first probe used a hand-rolled harness importing `'../src/index'` and produced `nodes: []`. Rather than report that, I ran their whole suite to establish the instrument was sound — 91 green — then rebuilt the probe on their harness. The `nodes: []` result reproduced. Only then did I treat it as a finding.)*

### 3b. Probe A — our extension, no resource

Input: one node, `position [100,100]`, `size [320,240]`, `data: [{@ocif/rect, fillColor:"#fffbe6"}, {@lar/tiddler, …}]`.

**What survived:** `@lar/tiddler` came back **byte-identical** — `uri`, the nested `fields` object, the `tags` array, all of it.

**What did not:**
- `fillColor: "#fffbe6"` → `"#000000"`; `strokeWidth` absent → `12`. tldraw quantises to its own palette and stroke sizes.
- `id: "n1"` → **`"shape:n1"`**. Every identifier rewritten.
- **No `schemas` entry for `@lar/tiddler` was emitted.** Export regenerates the schemas array from tldraw's own registry — `src/ocif.ts:324`: `const schemas = getOcifSchemas().filter(s => usedSchemaTypes.has(s.name) || s.name === 'ocif')` — so a foreign name never appears. **The output is therefore spec-invalid**: it uses an `@`-prefixed short name with no mapping, violating §Extension Mechanism's "that name must be present in the schemas section."

### 3c. Probe B — the same node, plus a `text/vnd.tiddlywiki` resource

Also supplying a `schemas` array declaring `@lar/tiddler`, and a canvas-level `data: [{type:"@lar/canvas", …}]`.

Output, complete:

```json
{ "ocif": "https://canvasprotocol.org/ocif/v0.7.0", "nodes": [] }
```

**Total loss.** Node, extension, geometry, text, canvas data, schemas — everything.

### 3d. Isolating the cause — one variable at a time

| input | shapes after import | nodes in export |
|---|---|---|
| node with `@ocif/rect`, **no resource** | 1 | 1 |
| + a `schemas` array | 1 | 1 |
| + canvas-level `data` | 1 | 1 |
| + **`text/vnd.tiddlywiki`** resource | **0** | **0** |
| + **`text/plain`** resource | **0** | **0** |
| + **`text/html`** resource | **0** | **0** |
| + `image/png` resource | 1 | 1 |
| + **dangling** resource ref (`resource:"nope"`, no `resources` array) | **0** | **0** |

`*` **That last row is the control that identifies the mechanism.** A dangling reference and an unsupported MIME type produce the *same* result, which tells us the drop is not keyed on MIME type at all — it is keyed on **"this node references a resource that yielded no tldraw asset."**

### 3e. The code path

`parseOcifFile` (`src/ocif.ts:381-395`) converts resources first and **only records an asset when the converter returns non-null**:

```ts
if (data.resources) {
  for (const resource of data.resources) {
    if (assetMap.has(resource.id)) continue
    const assetResult = convertOcifResourceToTldrawAsset(resource)
    if (assetResult) {
      records.push(assetResult.asset)
      assetMap.set(resource.id, assetResult.asset.id)
      …
    }
  }
}
```

`convertOcifResourceToTldrawAsset` (`src/ocif.ts:1171 ff.`) branches on MIME type — `image/*`, `video/*`, and `text/html`-with-bookmark-metadata — and otherwise falls through to a bare `return null`. So a text resource never enters `assetMap`. The node then fails to produce a shape, and the survivors are filtered again at `src/ocif.ts:680-704` ("Filter out any records that might be invalid or have broken references") before `createTLStore({snapshot})`.

Two spec-provided escape hatches are ignored on the way past:
1. **The representation fallback chain.** My probe supplied `text/plain` as a *second* representation behind the wikitext. §Fallback says later representations are fallbacks. The reader never consults it.
2. **The placeholder rectangle.** §Layout Properties: "a size property is useful as a fallback to **display a placeholder rectangle if the resource cannot be displayed as intended.**" The reader has `size: [320,240]` in hand and discards the node instead.

Note the asymmetry with the *extension* path, which is where the implementation is careful. `src/ocif.ts:1297-1313` defines `KNOWN_NODE_DATA_TYPES` (fifteen entries), `:1326` filters `unknownData`, `:1340` stashes it as `meta.ocifExtensions`, `:1700-1704` builds a placeholder geo shape flagged `ocifUnknownPrimary: true` — with the comment "For unknown types, create a placeholder geo shape. The original data is preserved in meta and restored verbatim on export" — and `:277-286` restores it:

```ts
// Re-emit extensions we don't understand, unchanged (OCIF requires
// unknown extensions to survive the round trip).
const preserved = Array.isArray(shape.meta?.ocifExtensions) ? shape.meta.ocifExtensions : null
if (preserved) {
  if (shape.meta?.ocifUnknownPrimary) {
    node.data = [...preserved]     // restore original, not the placeholder rect
  } else {
    node.data.push(...preserved)
  }
}
```

`!` **They built precisely the placeholder-and-preserve discipline for unknown extension *types* and did not build it for unresolvable *resources*.**

### 3f. The spec's own Hello World, run through it

I transcribed §Hello World Example verbatim — Berlin (rect, `text/plain` "Berlin"), Germany (oval, `text/plain` "Germany 🇩🇪"), and an arrow carrying both `@ocif/arrow` and `@ocif/edge` with `rel: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Property:P1376"` ("is capital of") — and round-tripped it.

Losses:
- **`berlin-node` is gone.** It had a `text/plain` resource.
- **Both text resources are gone.** The output has no `resources` key at all. The strings "Berlin" and "Germany 🇩🇪" no longer exist.
- **The edge lost `start`** (Berlin no longer exists to point at) **and lost `rel`** — the wikidata predicate, which is the entire semantic content of the example. Surviving edge: `{"type":"@ocif/edge","end":"shape:germany-node"}`.
- IDs rewritten to `shape:*`.
- Germany's `fillColor: "#FFFFFF"` → `"#FF0000"`; `strokeWidth: 5` → `8`.
- Separate probe: `rootNode`, node `comment`, and `parent` are all dropped.

Survivors: one oval, one arrow, and a regenerated `schemas` block.

### 3g. Why 91 tests are green

I inspected their fixture. `src/__tests__/smoke-test/QA pruned.ocif.json` — 13 nodes, 2 resources; extension types `@ocif/arrow, @ocif/edge, @ocif/path, @ocif/rect, @ocif/textstyle, @tldraw/node/bookmark, @tldraw/node/embed, @tldraw/node/note`; **MIME types present: `application/json`, `image/gif`, `text/html`** (the last only in the bookmark path).

**Their corpus contains no text node.** The suite is green because it never asks the question. *(Standing feedback, "collide the instrument": a hand-assembled fixture cannot notice what it omitted.)*

### 3h. `!` Ruling: requirement, ambiguity, or bug — and how I know

**Implementation bug. High confidence, 18/20.** Not a spec requirement, and not a spec ambiguity. Four independent lines of evidence:

1. **The spec explicitly prescribes the correct alternative.** §Layout Properties names the placeholder rectangle as the fallback "if the resource cannot be displayed as intended." Deletion is not the specified degradation; a box is.
2. **The spec explicitly normalises the input.** §"Text Nodes" states that a node displaying a `text/plain` resource is not a special case but *the* way text works in OCIF, and gives the exact shape my probe used. A reader that drops it is not resolving an ambiguity — it is failing the base case.
3. **The spec provides a second, independent recovery path that is also ignored.** §Fallback's ordered representations exist for precisely this. Two mechanisms, both bypassed.
4. **The implementation demonstrably knows better in the adjacent code.** It built placeholder-and-preserve for unknown extension types (`ocifUnknownPrimary`), with a comment citing the spec. The resource path lacks the same treatment. That is an omission, not a reading.

`_` **Why the distinction changes what we do.** If it were a spec requirement, OCIF would be unusable for us and the thread ends. If it were a spec ambiguity, the fix would be spec text and a working-group fight. Because it is an implementation bug in a small MIT repo with one author and one closed issue: **it is a filed bug and, if we choose, a pull request.** Roughly a dozen lines — return a placeholder shape carrying `meta.ocifResource` when `convertOcifResourceToTldrawAsset` yields null, and re-emit the resource on export. That is a *cheap* piece of goodwill and the fastest possible way to introduce ourselves to both tldraw and OCWG with something other than an opinion.

`!` But note what does **not** change: today, in the field, the loss is real. **We must not build any feature whose correctness depends on a foreign tool preserving our content.**

---

## 4. `_` `@lar/tiddler` — what I built, and what its survival does and does not prove

### 4a. What I constructed

Two artefacts. First, a **spec-shaped extension declaration** — a URI (`https://lares.example/ns/ocif/tiddler/1.0`), an `@`-prefixed short name (`@lar/tiddler`), and an inline JSON Schema, exactly as §Defining Extensions prescribes:

```json
{"uri":"https://lares.example/ns/ocif/tiddler/1.0","name":"@lar/tiddler",
 "schema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","title":"@lar/tiddler","type":"object",
   "properties":{"type":{"const":"@lar/tiddler"},"uri":{"type":"string"},
                 "fields":{"type":"object"},"tags":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},
   "required":["type","uri"]}}
```

Second, a **document that carries an actual tiddler four ways at once**, to test every carrier v0.7.0 opened:

- the extension **on a node** — `{"type":"@lar/tiddler", "uri":"lar:///the-door.names.the-whole-arc", "fields":{"title":"the-door","type":"text/vnd.tiddlywiki","modified":"20260902120000000","creator":"@joshu"}, "tags":["$:/tags/Canvas","spine"]}`;
- the wikitext **as a resource** with a three-step fallback chain — `text/vnd.tiddlywiki` (`"This is [[the door|the-door]] with a {{transclusion}} and a <<macro>>."`), then `text/html`, then `text/plain`;
- the extension **on a representation** (the v0.7.0 Entity widening);
- a lar-URI predicate **on an edge** — `{"type":"@ocif/edge","start":…,"end":…,"rel":"lar:///transcludes"}`;
- plus a canvas-level `data` entry.

### 4b. It validates

Against the official `spec/v0.7.0/schema.json`, with `jsonschema` 4.26 (`check_schema` first, then `iter_errors`): **0 errors.**

So: wikilink syntax, transclusion syntax, macro syntax, TiddlyWiki field names, `$:/`-prefixed system tags, a lar URI as an edge predicate, a custom MIME type, and a nested `fields` object all pass. §Extension Mechanism's "Arbitrary, nested JSON structures are allowed" is not marketing.

### 4c. `!` Why it survives byte-identical when the spec's own example does not

**Because they travel on different rails, and only one of them is defended.**

`@lar/tiddler` rides in `node.data` — the extension array. `tldraw/ocif` implements preserve-and-passthrough there deliberately: unknown `type`s are filtered out at import (`KNOWN_NODE_DATA_TYPES`), parked whole in `shape.meta.ocifExtensions`, and spliced back verbatim on export. Nothing inspects the payload; nothing normalises it; the round trip is a copy.

"Berlin" rides in `resources[]` — a *modelled* structure that the implementation tries to *understand*, mapping OCIF resources onto tldraw's asset types. Understanding is lossy where the mapping is partial, and the partial mapping fails closed by discarding.

`*` **The general law, and it is worth carrying beyond OCIF: the opaque channel is the reliable one. Anything a foreign tool tries to comprehend, it can fail to comprehend, and its failure mode is deletion.**

### 4d. `!` Is the survival a guarantee of the spec, or a property of that implementation?

**A property of that implementation. Explicitly not a guarantee of the spec.**

Chain of reasoning, each link cited above: the spec's only MUST about unknown extensions says they "MUST be ignored" (§Canvas Extensions); preservation appears as a lowercase "should" (§Data Extension) and as keyword-free narrative (§Handling Extension Data); there is no RFC 2119 clause, so none of these words carries defined force; there is no conformance section, so there is no such thing as a non-conforming consumer to appeal to; and there is no test suite, so nothing measures it.

`tldraw/ocif` preserves because Mime Čuvalo chose to and wrote a comment saying "OCIF requires unknown extensions to survive the round trip" — a claim about the spec that, having read every MUST in it, I can say is **not accurate**. The behaviour is right. Its stated justification is not in the document.

`_` **So the survival I measured is evidence about one implementation's good faith, not about the format's guarantees.** Any other tool may legally ignore and drop our extension and remain exactly as conformant. That is the single most important qualifier on the answer to question 9, and it is the thing an OCWG conformance section would fix.

---

## 5. `!` Round-trip fidelity and conformance

### 5a. What the spec says about lossless round-tripping

`design/goals.md` frames it as the whole point:

> "- Allow an application A to export data, - Let application B import it, - Let application B see the data, which reasonably resembles what a user in A saw (**goal: visual resemblance**), - Let application B make changes to the data… - Let application A import the data, **and have its special data still be there (goal: round-tripping).**
>
> - If a node has been deleted in app B, it should also be deleted for app A. - If a node has been added in B, no native data for app A can be present. However, app A can import the node and add some defaults."

And the honest limit, stated twice in identical words (§Layout Properties, §Global Positions):

> "When importing 3D as 2D, the z position is ignored… When a position is changed, the z position CAN be set to 0. **Yes, this implies that full round-tripping is not always possible.**"

Also relevant: `comment` "MAY be preserved or discarded when processing OCIF files, and in either case the file is functionally equivalent" — a deliberate, declared lossy channel.

### 5b. Is there a conformance suite? **No.**

- The `ocwg/.github` org profile still lists, under **"Possible outcomes"**: "Develop a **test bench** for the standard to demonstrate capabilities of infinite canvas tools. (Similar to the [**CSS Acid Tests**](https://www.acidtests.org/))". Two and a half years on, still aspirational.
- **CI is whitespace.** `.github/workflows/static_checks.yml` runs one job, `pre-commit/action@v3.0.1`; `.pre-commit-config.yaml` has one hook, `file_format.py`. The repo's own `CLAUDE.md`: "**The only automated check is a file format validator** run via pre-commit… This strips trailing whitespace, normalizes to LF line endings, removes BOM, and ensures a final newline." No schema validation. No example validation. No round-trip check.
- `ocwg/ocif-tools` `test-files/` contains **one** file (`jsoncanvas.ocif.json`).

### 5c. What `ocwg/ocif-lib` actually checks

Last commit **2025-08-12** (thirteen months). Nx monorepo: `libs/ocif-lib` plus apps `ocif-tools-app`, `jsoncanvas-ocif`, `nodejs-api`, `ocif-tools-app-e2e`.

**The library is a stub.** `libs/ocif-lib/src/lib/ocif-lib.ts`, in its entirety:

```ts
export function ocifLib(): string {
  return 'ocif-lib';
}
```

Its only test:

```ts
describe('ocifLib', () => {
  it('should work', () => {
    expect(ocifLib()).toEqual('ocif-lib');
  });
});
```

The substance is `libs/ocif-lib/src/schema/schema.ts` (180 lines) — a hardcoded schema whose `title` is **"OCIF core 0.5"**, still carrying `relations`. Three spec versions stale.

**The validator is Ajv against that stale schema and nothing more.** `apps/ocif-tools-app/src/services/validation-service.ts` (251 lines) is `new Ajv2020({allErrors:true, verbose:true}).compile(ocifCoreSchema)` plus ~200 lines of hand-rolled line/column mapping so errors can be pointed at in a textarea. No extension-schema resolution, no `schemas`-array cross-checking, no structural rules, no round-trip harness.

xamde confirmed the scope himself (Discussion #63): "**our current validator only does JSON Schema validation, no structural checks beyond that.**"

### 5d. What the `JSONCanvasToOCIF` converter checks

`ocwg/ocif-lib/apps/jsoncanvas-ocif` — a Vite/React browser app; `npm run serve:jsoncanvas-ocif`. It emits `canvasprotocol.org/ocif/**v0.5**` (`src/App.tsx:96`). A separate personal build, `devhelpr/jsoncanvas-to-ocif`, last pushed 2025-04-24. Sibling converters in `ocif-tools-app/src/services`: `jsoncanvas-service.ts` (116 lines), `svg-service.ts` (338), `tldraw-service.ts` (346).

`!` **It is a one-way format converter with no verification step.** It does not check round-trip; it does not check extension preservation; it emits a superseded version. Its output would fail nothing, because nothing validates against v0.7.0.

### 5e. Do they run? Yes — and that is the problem

| endpoint | HTTP |
|---|---|
| `https://validator.canvasprotocol.org/` | **200** |
| `https://ocif-validator.vercel.app/` | **200** |
| `https://jsoncanvas-to-ocif.vercel.app/` | **200** |

All live. All v0.5. A user validating a v0.7.0 file today gets a green light from a v0.5 checker.

### 5f. `!` The CR's schema URIs do not resolve to schemas

§Practical Recommendations: "A schema MUST have a URI as its identifier. A schema **SHOULD be hosted at its URI**," with a Cool-URIs content-negotiation recommendation (`Accept: application/json` → the schema; `text/html` → a human page).

Measured:

```
$ curl -sL https://spec.canvasprotocol.org/v0.7.0/extensions/rect.json
final = https://github.com/ocwg/ocif-spec/blob/main/spec/v0.7.0/extensions/rect.json
ctype = text/html; charset=utf-8
size  = 244547
→ json.decoder.JSONDecodeError
```

**244 kilobytes of GitHub chrome where a JSON Schema should be.** By design, not accident — `public/_redirects`:

```
/:version/extensions/:extension.json  https://github.com/ocwg/spec/blob/main/spec/:version/extensions/:extension.json  301
```

Pointed at `blob/` (the HTML view), not `raw.githubusercontent.com`. **Every `$ref` in the specification is unresolvable.** Any validator that follows extension URIs gets HTML. This is what Orion's still-open PR #68 and issue #54 were about; issue #54 ("Host spec and examples on Cloudflare rather than redirecting to GitHub repo") has been open since before the v0.7.0 release.

### 5g. `!` The spec's own examples are stale and nothing catches it

```
example/4x4-rect-node-grid.ocif.json    ocif=…ocif/v0.5    schema errors: 0
example/circle-node.json                ocif=…ocif/v0.5    schema errors: 0
example/cookbook-sticky-note.ocif.json  ocif=…ocif/v0.7.0  schema errors: 0
example/single-node.json                ocif=…ocif/v0.5    schema errors: 0
```

Three of four still declare v0.5. And every extension type string across all of them is the **retired** naming:

```
@ocif/node/rect      (×2, including the file labelled v0.7.0)
@ocif/node/oval
```

v0.7.0 renamed these to `@ocif/rect` / `@ocif/oval` and dropped the `-node`/`-rel` parts entirely. `cookbook.md`'s sticky-note recipe carries `@ocif/node/rect` too, and its Transclusion recipe is written against a **`relations` array** that no longer exists in v0.7.0, referencing `spec/v0.4/spec.md`. `catalog.md` lists every extension at v0.5 URIs.

The release procedure specifies the fix. `spec/v0.7.0/how-to-spec.md`, step 5: "Excluding the `/spec` directory, find and replace the previous version with the new version… This will update the **Cookbook, Catalog, Examples, and README.md**." It was not done. All four validate clean because the validator is on v0.5 and the schema does not check the version string.

`_` **Summary for question 5: there is no conformance suite, the reference library is a stub, the reference validator is three versions stale and checks only shape, the converter is one-way and unverified, the extension URIs serve HTML, and the spec's own examples are wrong. None of this is hidden — it is all visible in the repo — but none of it is caught, because nothing checks anything.**

---

## 6. `?` Governance and participation

### 6a. Cadence and venue

**Bi-weekly, Tuesdays.** `canvasprotocol.org` lists 42 meetings from #1 (2024-03-26) through #42 (2026-03-24), with #43 announced for **2026-04-07, 12:00 ET** via Luma (`luma.com/6usys17z`). Calls are recorded to YouTube and transcribed into `ocwg/discussions/meetings/`.

Channels, all public except one:
- **GitHub Discussions** — `github.com/orgs/ocwg/discussions`, the stated Feedback address in the spec header. 70 discussions.
- **Discord** — `discord.gg/32CWVJkUts`. `!` Auth-walled; I could not read it, and meeting notes reference substantive spec discussion happening there.
- **Buttondown newsletter** — `buttondown.com/ocwg`.
- **GitHub repos** — "All major discussions and artefacts are public on GitHub."

### 6b. How decisions are made

`design/design-decisions.md` states the process, such as it is: "The design decisions document tries to capture the consensus reached in the discussions, **both the GitHub discussions and the bi-weekly calls.** Some decisions are still not represented in the spec."

Decisions land as prose rulings with rationale — e.g. list-vs-map for `schemas` ("**Decision**: We use lists," reasoned from JSON-parser error quality on hand-merged files); keeping `type` as the reserved key ("**Decision**: We keep `type`, as it is so short and clearly expresses the intent"); the v0.5 coordinate split, tagged "Discussed in GitHub #52 and Meeting 025."

`!` **There is no governance document, no charter, no membership roll, no voting rule, no IPR or patent policy, no editor-succession plan, and no appeals path.** Jess Martin said so plainly in Meeting 9: "Orion Reed and I had the idea for this several months ago and initiated it and **there is no legal structure whatsoever.**"

Nor is participation load-bearing on anyone's employment. Orion, Meeting 1:

> "**No one's paying you to be here. You're turning up of your own volition**… [we want] getting stuff working as quickly as we can so that we can share this sense of progress being made and **not turn into a decades-long bureaucratic standards body.**"

`_` `0` For a format that wants to be an interchange standard between commercial products, the absence of an IPR policy is the unsettled item I would flag hardest to the operator. Contributing spec text to a body with no patent or copyright policy, under CC BY-SA, is a boundary decision and not mine to make.

### 6c. How an implementer joins, and what they owe

Joining is showing up: click the Discord invite, open a Discussion, join a Tuesday call, or open a PR. No application, no fee, no sponsor.

What you owe is stated in exactly two places, and both say the same thing. Spec repo `README.md`:

> "**⚒️ Status: Seeking Implementation Feedback** — The spec is in the *'Candidate Recommendation'* stage, which means it has been widely reviewed and satisfies OCWG's technical requirements. **We are currently gathering implementation experience.** We welcome any feedback and implementations."

Org profile, "Get Involved": "📝 Read the most recent spec. 🛠️ **Implement the spec and give us feedback!** 📣 Read and participate in the GitHub Discussions."

The v0.7.0 announcement closes the same way: "**We're seeking implementation feedback** — if you're building with OCIF or considering it, we'd love to hear from you."

`*` **We are, precisely, the constituency the CR is asking for. Implementation experience is the whole ask, and we would arrive with measurements.**

### 6d. Who is actually doing the work

`ocwg/ocif-spec` contributors, complete list, ever:

```
202  xamde        (Dr. Max Völkel, editor)
 86  jessmartin
 22  devhelpr     (Maikel van de Lisdonk)
  8  aaronfranke  (Aaron Franke)
```

**Four humans. And OrionReed has zero commits to the spec repo**, despite being a listed author on the title page. `ocwg/discussions`: devhelpr 35, jessmartin 10, OrionReed 7. `ocwg/ocif-lib`: devhelpr 16, jessmartin 3.

His complete OCWG issue/PR footprint across the org, by `search/issues?q=org:ocwg+involves:OrionReed` — ten items in two and a half years, and **every one is infrastructure, hosting, or copy-editing**: `ocif-spec#68` (vitepress spec site, **open**), `ocif-spec#67` (formatting fix on the size property, **open**), `.github#1` (update org README to CR, **open**), `canvasprotocol.org#8` (site to CR), `ocif-spec#18` (migrate to Cloudflare Pages), `canvasprotocol.org#6` (redirect), `ocif-spec#7` (Spec 0.2.1), `canvasprotocol.org#3/#2/#1` (meeting link, copy edits). **He has never opened a PR against the data model.**

Repo statistics: 51 stars, 2 forks, 4 watchers, 16 open issues. Tags: `v0.7.0`, `0.6`. Branches: `main`, `v0.7.1-draft`, `spec-page`, `OrionReed-patch-1`.

### 6e. `!` My ruling on the quiet

Every public surface, measured 2026-09-02:

| surface | last activity | gap |
|---|---|---|
| `ocif-spec` pushes | **2026-04-10** | 4.8 months |
| `v0.7.1-draft` branch head | 2026-03-24 | prepared, never released |
| `spec-page` branch (Orion's) | 2026-03-24 | PR #68 open |
| Buttondown newsletter | 2026-03-24 | 5.3 months |
| `canvasprotocol.org` | 2026-03-24 | still advertises #43/Apr 7 as "next" |
| New Discussion opened | 2026-04-05 (#70) | 5 months |
| Any Discussion comment | 2026-07-12 (a reply on #39, *Wikipedia Entry*) | 1.7 months |
| **Meeting summary published** | **#39, 2026-01-20** | **7.5 months** |
| `ocif-lib` | 2025-08-12 | 12.7 months |

Meeting summaries in `ocwg/discussions/meetings/`: 029, 030, 033, 034, 035, 039. **Numbers 031, 032, 036, 037, 038 and everything after 039 were never published**, even though the website lists meetings through #43.

**Ruling: a stall of the public process, not death of the work..** I hold it at 15 because the evidence genuinely cuts both ways and I want you to see both edges.

*For stall:* a v0.7.1 draft prepared 2026-03-24, accepted by the editor on 2026-04-10 ("Done. Thanks again for the detailed, thoughtful proposal"), and **never released** five months later; both of Orion's PRs open since March; the release checklist half-executed (catalog, cookbook and examples never updated); nine of the last fifteen meetings unrecorded; the website's "next meeting" five months in the past.

*Against death:* **Max Völkel's own Java implementation `Calpano/graphinout/reader-ocif07` was pushed 2026-07-31** and its README states it "targets the ***v0.7.1-draft*** spec (and still reads v0.7.0 and v0.6 documents for backwards compatibility). **It emits v0.7.1 by default**… The v0.7.1 change relative to v0.7.0 — a node `resource` may be an inline Resource object instead of a string id — is supported." The editor never stopped. The *working group's publishing* stopped.

`_` And Meeting #39's own agenda (2026-01-20) tells you where the attention went. Its topics: spec hosting for LLM consumption; whether to ship a separate "LLM.txt"; "**LLM as Main Reader of the Specification**"; testing LLM-generated converters by round-tripping real files A→OCIF→A; anticipation of Gemini 3 for library generation; and integrating Codex/Claude for PR review. Meeting #35 records the same: "Discussion centered on the insight that **large language models (LLMs) will be the primary reader of the specification.**" A group that has decided its primary reader is a model has, in effect, decided that human implementer recruitment is someone else's job.

One human-oversight note from #35 worth keeping: "**A viewport canvas schema bug was found that LLMs didn't catch**, highlighting the ongoing importance of human review alongside LLM automation."

