## 7. `_` Who implements OCIF today

GitHub code search for `"canvasprotocol.org/ocif"` (89 hits) plus `"@ocif/rect"` and `"ocif.json"`, deduplicated to repositories, non-OCWG only:

| repo | last push | licence | ★ | what it is |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **`tldraw/ocif`** | **2026-08-10** | **MIT** | 0 | tldraw v5.3 ⇄ OCIF v0.7.0. Real, 91 tests, extension-preserving. **Not shipped in tldraw.** |
| `Calpano/graphinout` | 2026-07-31 | NOASSERTION | 15 | Max Völkel's Java **reader** (OCIF→GraphML), targets v0.7.1-draft |
| `dundalek/stratify` | 2026-02-15 | MIT | 112 | codebase-architecture visualiser; OCIF one of several outputs |
| `abstractalgo/graflint` | 2026-01-06 | none | 0 | OCIF linter POC, born from Discussion #63 |
| `chee/Littlebook` | 2025-08-11 | AGPL-3.0+ | 2 | **OCIF-in-Automerge with two pluggable views — see 7c** |
| `devhelpr/code-flow-canvas` | 2025-11-17 | NOASSERTION | 5 | Maikel's visual-programming canvas (spec co-author) |
| `aaronfranke/godot-ocif-…` | 2025-07-08 | Unlicense | 0 | Godot addon (spec co-author) |
| `jmsv/ocif-thing` | 2025-06-13 | none | 2 | small OCIF editor |
| `devhelpr/jsoncanvas-to-ocif` | 2025-04-24 | none | 0 | converter |
| `RonHannis/ACForms` | — | — | — | incidental `ocif.json` filename hit |

**Two of the nine are the spec's own authors implementing their own spec.** Four are stale by six months or more. One is a linter. `Calpano/graphinout` is a *reader*, not a round-tripper — its own audit file notes "**Unknown relation types are only logged**."

### 7a. `!` Who does *not* implement it

Absent from every search: **tldraw (shipping), Excalidraw, Obsidian, Miro, Kinopio, Heptabase, Muse, Notion, Roam, draw.io, Zwibbler, infinitymaps, Kosmik, yEd.** Every app named in `design/goals.md`'s own target list. **Zero commercial canvas applications ship OCIF import or export.**

### 7b. `!` What `tldraw/ocif` being the only maintained implementation implies

The provenance is the whole story. `tldraw/tldraw#6293`, "feat(ocif): implement OCIF spec for import and export," opened 2025-06-20 by Mime Čuvalo. His own framing: "this is me **vibe coding**, pushing Cursor + Codex to see if we can implement the OCIF spec… still a WIP!"

Steve Ruiz, tldraw's founder, same day:

> "This would probably be best as a .tldr -> .ocif converter, maybe even a package. **I wouldn't add it to the main .com or tldraw packages.** (We probably shouldn't have the excalidraw stuff in there either)
>
> let's make this a **nights and weekend issue**, ocif is interesting but **I don't want to spend a dollar of engineering time on it** :("

Mime: "fair enough! yeah, i'm just mucking about for this one, not treating it seriously. more about using something to figure out Cursor+Codex. **Maybe when OCIF gets to 1.0 we'll look at it more seriously.**"

Orion, in that same thread — his only substantive public OCIF advocacy I found:

> "If you do have bandwidth pre-1.0 **it's super helpful getting feedback on the spec** (the stuff in Discord was already great!). We want to make sure that 1.0 doesn't have any large issues and so **now is the best time to make potentially large changes pre-1.0 if they're called for.**"

**Closed unmerged 2026-02-04** by steveruizok ("Closing this one"). Mime: "i'll move this to a separate repo (off-hours) as discussed offline before." `tldraw/ocif` created two days later.

**What this implies, in four parts:**

1. `!` **OCIF is not in any shipping product, including the one whose org hosts the best implementation.** It was explicitly refused entry to the main packages by the founder, on cost grounds.
2. `_` **The most active implementation is one engineer's off-hours work**, sustained across fourteen months and two repos — which is genuine commitment, but it is one person and one closed issue deep. Its bus factor is one.
3. `*` **The licence posture is more favourable than it looks, in exactly one direction.** `tldraw/ocif` ships its own `LICENSE` — "MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 tldraw" — while `tldraw` itself is a `peerDependency` under the tldraw licence (`node_modules/tldraw/LICENSE.md`: "This code is licensed under the tldraw license"). So the **runtime** remains disqualified for us on the grounds already established, but `src/ocif.ts` — 2,279 lines of OCIF v0.7.0 codec with real extension-preservation machinery, plus 2,598 lines of tests — **is MIT and is legible, citable, and adaptable by us.** That is the single most valuable free artefact this whole investigation turned up: a worked reference implementation of the spec, under a licence we can read from, whose defects I have already located.
4. `!` **"Maybe when OCIF gets to 1.0" is a conditional that nobody is currently working to satisfy.** v0.7.1 has sat unreleased since April. The largest potential adopter is waiting on a milestone the working group has stopped visibly moving toward.

### 7c. `*` `chee/Littlebook` — the closest prior art to what we are contemplating

This deserves its own reading. `plugins/opencanvas`, AGPL-3.0+, author chee:

```json
"dependencies": {
  "@automerge/automerge": "3.0.0-beta.0",
  "@automerge/vanillajs": "2.0.0",
  "@excalidraw/excalidraw": "0.18.0",
  "@tldraw/editor": "^3.7.2", "@tldraw/tldraw": "^2.3.0", "@tldraw/tlschema": "^2.3.0"
}
```

Structure: `src/sources/createOpenCanvas.ts` mints an **OCIF document as the Automerge doc content** — complete with an inline `schemas` entry declaring a custom `@ocif/circle` extension with its own JSON Schema. Then `src/views/tldraw/{from,to}-opencanvas.ts` and `src/views/excalidraw/{from,to}-opencanvas.ts` render **the same document through two different canvas engines**, plus a `views/readonly/`. Reconciliation is `src/utils/patch.ts`, six lines:

```ts
import {compare, applyPatch} from "fast-json-patch"
export function patch<T extends object | Array<any>>(oldObj: T, newObj: T) {
  const patch = compare(oldObj, newObj)
  applyPatch(oldObj, patch, false, true)
}
```

`!` That diff-and-apply is precisely the "generic merge" xamde named as the missing piece — attempted naively (whole-document JSON-patch against a CRDT, no extension-aware preservation, no identity tracking). It targets **OCIF v0.4** and is stale since 2025-08-11.

`*` **But the architecture is ours: an OCIF document living in Automerge as the source of truth, with pluggable renderers over it.** And chee is Patchwork's top contributor. The thread we are pulling is already tied at the other end.

