## 8. Orion Reed's technical models

### 8a. `?` Scoped Propagators

**Sources.** Essay: `https://orionreed.com/posts/scoped-propagators/`, 2024-07-08. Talk at **LIVE 2024 / SPLASH**, 20 min, Substrates session. Code: `github.com/OrionReed/scoped-propagators`; demo `orionreed.github.io/scoped-propagators/`. `!` **No proceedings paper and no DOI — the essay is the artifact.** His own BibTeX entry, which I found inside folkjs (`website/live-2025/folkjs.bib`, key `Reed2024`), states the thesis compactly:

> "Graphs, as a model of computation and as a means of interaction and authorship, have found success in specific domains such as shader programming and signal processing. In these systems, computation is often expressed on nodes of specific types, with edges representing the flow of information. This is a powerful and general-purpose model, but is typically a **closed-world environment where both node and edge types are decided at design-time. By choosing an alternate topology where computation is represented by edges, the incentive for a closed environment is reduced.** I present Scoped Propagators (SPs), a programming model designed to be **embedded within existing environments and user interfaces**. By representing computation as mappings between nodes along edges, SPs make it possible to **add behaviour and interactivity to environments which were not designed with liveness in mind.** I demonstrate an implementation of the SP model in an infinite canvas environment, where users can create arrows between arbitrary shapes and define SPs as **Javascript object literals on these arrows.**"

**Lineage, in his words.** Radul's 2009 MIT dissertation *Propagation Networks: A Flexible and Expressive Substrate for Computation* (Sussman advising), and Steele's constraint work. He states the three deltas himself:

> "Scoped Propagators are related to **Propagator Networks** but differ in three key ways: 1. propagation happens along **edges** instead of **nodes**; 2. propagation is only fired when a **scope condition** is met; 3. instead of **stateful cell nodes** and **propagator nodes**, **all nodes can be stateful and can be of an arbitrary type.**"

**The model concretely.** A scoped propagator is "a function which takes a source and target node and returns a **partial update** to the target node," paired with "a **scope** which defines some subset of events which trigger propagation." Four scopes shipped: `change` (default — fires when the source changes), `click`, `tick` (every frame), `geo` (fires when a geometrically overlapping node changes). Syntax is a scope keyword followed by a JavaScript object literal with `from` and `to` in lexical scope:

```
click {x: from.x + 10, rotation: to.rotation + 1}
```

**The two insights, verbatim — this is the language you asked me to find:**

> "the Scoped Propagator model is based on two key insights: 1. **by representing computation as mappings between nodes along edges, you do not need to know at design-time what node types exist.** 2. by scoping the propagation to events, you can augment nodes with interactive behaviour suitable for the environment in which SPs have been embedded."

> "Scoped Propagators are interesting in part because of their **ability to cross the boundaries of otherwise siloed systems and to do so without the use of an escape hatch** — all additional behaviour happens **in-situ**, in the same environment, and not from editing source code."

> "**100% of the behaviour is encoded in the text of the arrows. This creates a kind of diagrammatic specification of behaviour, where all behaviours could be re-created from a static screenshot.**"

**Termination, cycles, merge — there is none, and he says so.** Essay, Open Questions:

> "**This model has not yet been formalised**, and while the propagators themselves can be simply expressed as a function `f(a,b) ↦ b'`, **I have not yet found an appropriate way to express scopes and the relationship between the two.**"

Repo README, Current Issues: "**cycles of `change` propagators cause infinite recursion.**" The source carries a literal `// TODO: handle cycles`. Merge is last-write-wins object spread — `editor.updateShape(unpack({...toShapePacked, ...result}))` — **not a lattice join**. Error handling is cosmetic: a throwing propagator turns its arrow orange.

**Is it implemented? Four generations, and the arc is the answer.**

1. **`scoped-propagators` (2024)** — ~840 LOC, 9 commits, **dead since 2024-07-09**, `"private": true`, pinned to tldraw 2.2.1. A demo, and honestly labelled as one.
2. **folkjs `<folk-event-propagator>` (2025)** — shipped, ~12 demos, the model's most-used incarnation. **But `grep -rn "scope" packages/propagators/src/` returns nothing.** I read all four classes — `Propagator` (198 lines), `BidirectionalPropagator` (293), `SymmetricPropagator` (256), `AsyncPropagator` (262). They are **event relays**: a `source`/`target` pair of `EventTarget`s, an event name, and a handler compiled from a string body via `new Function('from','to','event', functionBody)`. Cycle-breaking is a boolean, `#isPropagationLocked`, set around dispatch. **No cells, no lattice, no merge, no fixpoint, and — decisively — no scopes.** The four scope kinds were replaced by an arbitrary DOM event name in a `trigger` attribute. `!` **The distinguishing feature did not survive its own port.**
3. **`bireactive` (npm, v0.3.5, 2026-07-03)** — where the missing theory finally got built, in a *different* codebase. `src/propagators/lattice.ts`: "Because `meet` only narrows and lattices here have well-founded descent… **fixpoint iteration TERMINATES by construction. No fuel cap, no divergence panic.**" `src/propagators/solver.ts`: "**There is no `DivergedError`: monotone narrowing cannot diverge, only slow-converge.**" But these are **classical Radul propagators** — declared reads and writes, `merge()` by narrowing. **Edges-as-computation and scopes do not appear.**
4. **`@inkandswitch/edge-handles` (2026)** — the edge made persistent and first-class. README:

> "A **doc-backed reactive cell with named upstream and downstream connections** — the minimum primitive you need to build dataflow systems **where the wires themselves are first-class, sharable, persistent documents.** EdgeHandles are deliberately **not** transformers. They are the shared primitive on which transformers, **propagators**, and other dataflow systems can be built."

**`!` My judgement, since you asked for it directly: elegant, and the author's own subsequent work is the evidence.**

The *topological* insight is genuinely right, and it is the one thing we should take: **put the coupling on the relation, and you stop needing a closed type registry.** That is the same insight that makes OCIF's extension bag work (§2), the same one behind `folk-base-connection`'s addressable endpoints, and the same one behind our own `#has` capability edges. It is a real contribution and it generalises.

The *implementation* model does not hold up. Imperative last-write-wins partial updates, fired by ad-hoc event scopes, with no termination proof, no confluence, no merge semantics, and an acknowledged infinite-recursion bug — that is a demo-grade evaluator, and the essay says as much in its own Open Questions. The decisive evidence is what he did next: **he abandoned all three distinguishing features.** Scopes vanished in folkjs. Edges-as-computation vanished in bireactive, where he went and fetched the orthodox lattice. What he *kept* is edges-as-first-class-persistent-objects, in edge-handles.

**How it composes with an Automerge source of truth — badly, in its 2024 form, and `edge-handles` exists because of that.** A last-write-wins object spread over a shared replica is a merge-conflict generator: two peers firing the same propagator produce two different `updateShape` calls with no join, and Automerge will happily merge them into something neither peer computed. `_` **If we want this shape, we want `bireactive`'s lattice — monotone narrowing, which composes with a CRDT *because it is order-independent by construction* — carried on `edge-handles`' persistent edges. Not Scoped Propagators.** Worth noting the cycle problem is still open at the lab too: `patchwork-system/core/filesystem/directory-map.md` says "**cycle detection isn't done here. the service worker has a 5s timeout; that's the backstop.**"

### 8b. `_` folkjs at source

MIT, "Copyright 2024 Chris Shank." **Last commit `5e380e8d`, 2026-05-19**, sole branch `main`, 1290 commits. Velocity: 2026-03 → 78 commits, 2026-04 → 33, 2026-05 → 3. **Paused, confirmed.** The README's "It hasn't been published to NPM yet" is stale — all seven public packages sit at `0.0.10`, last published **2026-01-27**.

**`FolkSpace`** — `packages/labs/src/folk-space.ts`, 515 lines. A `ReactiveElement` whose shadow root is `<div class="space-content"><slot></slot></div>`, so **light-DOM children stay in the author's tree** and are projected into a transformed container. One composite CSS transform, set imperatively per microtask:

```ts
this.#contentElement.style.transform = M.toCSSString(this.matrix)
// matrix = scaleSelf(fromTranslate(x, y), scale, scale)
```

Not `zoom`, not container queries — a single `matrix()` string on one element. Pointer mapping is an `IPointTransform` interface (`mapPointFromParent`, `mapPointToParent`, `mapVectorFromParent`, …) by matrix inversion, with vectors deliberately dropping translation. Pinch-zoom is detected by `event.ctrlKey` on wheel; Firefox's line-delta mode is compensated. `#shouldStartPanning` **bails if any ancestor is scrollable** — the explicit concession that real DOM inside still needs to scroll. Scale is clamped to `[0.05, 8]`. `TransformStack.fromElement` walks ancestors so **spaces nest correctly**; in practice a bubbling `ShapeConnectedEvent` registers each ancestor space. The grid is five stacked `radial-gradient` layers driven by three custom properties — an LOD grid at ~zero per-frame JS cost.

**`ShiftingOriginGraph`** — `packages/labs/src/ShiftingOriginGraph.ts`, 312 lines. File header: `@note This is a PoC, lots of optimisations are possible.` **Only two consumers, both demos.** Nothing in `packages/` depends on it.

*The algorithm.* Extends `MultiGraph`. A `ZoomNode` carries opaque `data`; **every directed edge carries a `DOMMatrix`** describing how the target sits inside the source. Cycles are the point — an infinite zoom is a cycle you traverse forever. State is `originNodeId`, `#originTransform`, and `#maxNodes = 30`. Zooming accumulates into `#originTransform`; when a threshold trips, it re-bases by **at most one hop**:

```ts
#shiftOrigin(edge) {
  this.originNodeId = edge.target;
  // Before: viewport * edge = how target node appears
  // After: new target node is at origin (0,0)
  this.#originTransform = this.#originTransform.multiply(edge.transform);
}
```

*The threshold lives in the caller, not the class* — re-base when the candidate node **fully covers the viewport**, tested by `isScreenCoveredByRectangle`, which **samples** points rather than testing exactly. The demos additionally cap accumulated scale at 100× and reset to 1.0 on each re-base; **that, not the matrix, is what actually bounds float error.**

*Cost.* `getUnfoldedTree()` re-runs from scratch every frame with no memoisation. Two `DOMMatrix` allocations per edge per frame — `.multiply` is non-mutating and `multiplySelf` is never used. The traversal has **no visited set and no depth limit**; only the culling callback and a hard `maxNodes` counter stop an infinite walk through a cyclic graph.

*What breaks.* `#shiftOrigin`/`#unshiftOrigin` are `M·E` and `M·E⁻¹` using a numerically-computed inverse, with **no re-orthogonalisation — so zoom-in-then-out is not the identity.** Shift and unshift predicates are exact complements with **no dead band**, so the boundary flaps. Zoom-in takes the **first matching edge**; zoom-out takes `getSourceNodes(...)[0]` — **there is no path history, so in a multi-parent graph, zooming out lands you somewhere you have never been.** Culling is the caller's responsibility, assumes uniform scale, and **prunes the whole subtree** — a small off-screen node containing a huge zoomed-in child is dropped with it. And critically: **nothing renders truly nested DOM.** Every visible node is a flat sibling positioned by a composed CSS string — no containment, no parent `overflow:hidden` clipping, no depth z-ordering, and a hard LOD pop at `scale < 0.005`. Link positions are measured once at scale 1 and cached, **never invalidated on resize or content change**.

`!` **For us: the idea is sound and cheaply reimplementable in ~50 lines. The code is not a foundation.**

**`packages/collab/src/automerge.ts` — read in full, it is two lines:**

```ts
export { getBackend } from '@automerge/automerge';
export * from '@automerge/vanillajs';
```

`@folkjs/collab` is **not an Automerge binding.** It is a wasm-bundling re-export shim that surfaces `getBackend` (needed to resolve objId→path). It pins `@automerge/automerge ^3.2.3` and `@automerge/vanillajs ^2.3.0` — **not `automerge-repo` directly.** Anyone citing this file as folkjs's CRDT integration has cited the wrong file.

**The real binding is `packages/labs/src/folk-sync-attribute.ts`, 470 lines, and it is far more interesting to us.** Its CRDT schema is **HAST** — the unified HTML AST:

```ts
interface SyncElement extends Omit<HAST.Element, 'properties' | 'children'> {
  properties: { [key: string]: ImmutableString };
  children: SyncNode[];
}
```

So it syncs **arbitrary DOM subtrees** — every attribute, all text, all comments, full child order. Geometry rides along *only* because `folk-shape`'s state literally *is* an attribute string. The schema choice is deliberate and correct: **prose merges character-wise (plain `value` on text nodes), attributes are whole-value LWW (`ImmutableString`)** — you do not want two peers character-merging `"x: 10; y: 20"`. Identity is a `BiMap<DOMNode, ObjID>` resolved through `getBackend(doc).objInfo(id).path`. DOM→CRDT is a `MutationObserver` with `{attributes, characterData, childList, subtree}`; the childList handler pre-computes indices "to avoid O(n²) when inserting multiple nodes."

`!` **Three defects to know before borrowing it.**
1. `#applyRemotePatches` **disconnects the MutationObserver** while applying patches — the code explains why: "MutationObserver callbacks are async (microtasks), so a flag-based approach doesn't work." But **disconnecting discards queued records**, so a genuine local edit that lands during patch application is **silently lost**. That is a structural race, not a nit.
2. Only `insert` and `del` patch actions on children are handled; `splice`, `put`, `inc` are silently ignored.
3. Transport is hardcoded `new Repo({network: [new WebSocketClientAdapter('wss://sync.automerge.org')]})` — **no storage adapter, so nothing persists**, no auth, no rooms. Presence is dead code: `folk-presence.ts` is 670 lines of which 562 are commented out.

**`folk-shape-attribute` / `folk-space-attribute` — why a custom *attribute*.**

*Mechanism.* There is no native custom-attribute API, so `packages/dom/src/CustomAttribute.ts` (219 lines, "Rewritten from lume/custom-attributes") builds one: a single **document-wide `MutationObserver`** whose `attributeFilter` is re-derived on every `define()`, a `WeakMap<Element, Map<string, CustomAttribute>>` registry, a hand-rolled lifecycle (`connectedCallback` / `disconnectedCallback` / `changedCallback` / `connectedMoveCallback`), and an explicit upgrade sweep `node.querySelectorAll('[' + name + ']')` for elements already in the tree. It handles both Chrome dialects of `moveBefore()` and guards a cross-bundle singleton on `window.__customAttributes`.

Attribute values are a CSS-declaration mini-language, **parsed and written back**, so the DOM is the source of truth — which is precisely why `folk-sync` gets geometry sync for free. And one genuinely clever trick, selecting CSS on a substring of the value:

```css
[folk-shape*='x:'][folk-shape*='y:'] {
  position: absolute;
  left: var(--folk-x) !important;
  top:  var(--folk-y) !important;
}
```

so an unpositioned shape stays in normal document flow and only becomes absolutely positioned once `x`/`y` appear in the value.

*Why attributes and not wrappers — stated, in the LIVE 2025 paper in-repo* (`website/live-2025/live.md`, Shank & Reed):

> "While web-based systems often break when their DOM tree structure is modified, **they often tolerate the addition of _new_ attributes** that encode interactive functionality. **This tolerance creates one path for escaping isolated environments** — annotations can introduce liveness without requiring users to abandon their tools or migrate their work."

Orion, folkjs Discussion #36: "we can **augment existing DOM tree with spatial canvas behaviors without needing to modify the tree structure**, quite useful in adversarial environments!"

*What it buys.* It works on elements you cannot wrap (`<h1 folk-shape>`, `folk-camera` on a `<video>`). **No wrapper node**, so host CSS selectors, `:nth-child`, and flex/grid parent-child relationships all survive untouched. Multiple orthogonal behaviours compose on one element (`<div folk-shape folk-sync>`). State is serialisable **in the DOM itself**, which is what makes CRDT sync fall out for free. And there is no custom-element upgrade-timing hazard.

*The hard ceiling.* `FolkSpaceAttribute.connectedCallback` must `attachShadow` on someone else's element — the code's own comment: "**this attribute can only be added on elements without an existing shadowDOM**… If this attribute is removed then reapplied then it will error out." So `folk-space` **cannot be applied to any custom element at all.** Styles are injected globally via `document.adoptedStyleSheets` with a standing `// TODO: detect how to inject styles into shadowroot`. Value parsing is `split(';')`/`split(':')` with no escaping, so no value may contain those characters. There is always one frame where the attribute is present and its effect is not. And `FolkSpaceAttribute` clamps scale **only in CSS**, so at the zoom limits `mapPointFromParent()` disagrees with what is on screen — **hit-testing goes wrong exactly when the user is fully zoomed in or out.**

*Also worth your attention.* `folk-base-connection.ts` makes connection endpoints **addresses, not object references**, round-tripping three kinds through one attribute value: `::point(x, y)`, a serialised DOM `Range`, or a CSS selector. `*` **That is free addressability made literal — you can point an arrow at a text selection inside someone else's paragraph.** `folk-xanadu.ts` draws Xanadu-style transclusion ribbons between two Ranges by unioning `getClientRects()` into a `clip-path`. The spatial index is a `BoundingVolumeHierarchy` that is **fully rebuilt on every shape move**, and nothing in `packages/` culls. Nothing TiddlyWiki-shaped exists — **no source found** for transclusion macros, nested documents, or wikitext.

### 8c. `_` Ink & Switch — what the current work is

**`inkandswitch/patchwork-system`** (provenance already with you). `@patchwork/monorepo`, "A malleable software environment for collaborative work." pnpm workspaces + changesets, ~2300 commits, npm releases every few days, Playwright e2e suites named `concurrent-edits`, `cross-profile-sync`, `offline`, `reload-persistence`.

Automerge is central and **pinned exactly**: `@automerge/automerge 3.3.2`, `@automerge/automerge-repo 2.6.0-subduction.47`, `@automerge/automerge-repo-keyhive 0.3.0-alpha.sub.8b`, `@automerge/automerge-subduction 0.16.1`, `@keyhive/keyhive 0.1.0-alpha.5`. Their `AGENTS.md` explains why, and it is a lesson we have paid for ourselves:

> "These are **exact pins, not ranges**. Two of our packages published against different pins means **two copies of automerge-repo in the consumer's tree, which breaks document handle identity.**"

Project framing: "Patchwork is an exploration of **version control for writing and beyond**… Perhaps this version control could one day be built into the storage layer of your OS." Research areas: Universal Version Control, Malleable Software, Local-first.

**`inkandswitch/gaios.sgai.uk`** — created 2026-08-04, pushed 2026-09-02, no README. It is a **deployed Patchwork site**: roughly 35 lines calling `setup({packageListURL, accountKey: "gaiosAccountUrl", title: "GAIOS"})` against `@inkandswitch/patchwork ^0.7.3`, with a `KEYHIVE` sync-server switch. `sgai.uk` is ARIA's **Safeguarded AI** programme. Dispatch 014:

> "we are building **GAIOS, a malleable, collaborative software platform derived from our Patchwork project**… with **interoperability and version control powered by Automerge.**"

`_` Its `public/modules.json` tool list includes **`tldraw4` and `tldraw5`** alongside `codemirror-*`, `hazel`, `petrinaut`, and CatColab.

**`patchwork-system` bears directly on our stack** — it is the nearest live system to ours: Automerge 3.x plus Keyhive, a doc-URL-addressed virtual filesystem, tools-as-modules, view composition. Neither it nor GAIOS is a spatial canvas *per se*; the spatial work lives in `edge-handles` and in the Breadboard / Space-frame prototypes below.

**Liberatory Computing** — `libcomp.org`, which he co-wrote:

> "**LC is a loose collective of researchers, artists, and activists working to liberate computing from capitalism.** … By separating **production** from **use**, producing **inflexible software**, and slicing up computing into **siloed apps**, your agency is held back by a tech industry that profits from a population **rendered computationally passive.**"

### 8d. `*` His writing on the DOM as a spatial medium — quoted

**Why the field keeps failing.** LIVE 2025, `folkjs.org/live-2025/`:

> "Live programming research gravitates towards the creation of **isolated environments whose success is measured by domination: achieving adoption by displacing rather than integrating with existing tools and practices.**"

> "This inward focus produces systems which can be operated on from within themselves, but **neglect their participation in wider contexts of use**… Whereas traditional programming leverages **ubiquitous plaintext infrastructures that resist single-system dominance** through their simplicity and interoperability, **live programming's visual requirements largely preclude utilizing this pluralistic foundation.**"

**The DOM as substrate.**

> "**Free addressability** embraces transparent, publicly addressable state through queries, selectors, names, or other mechanisms that **make parts of a running system targetable from the outside without requiring permission or coordination from the original creators.**"

> "Our adversarial strategies exploit the fact that **user interfaces often expose more addressable surfaces than the underlying program** — through DOM elements, accessibility trees, and visual components."

> "**What enables all of these approaches to work is that the DOM operates as a running, shared representation of the UI; it would be nigh impossible to do this if pixels were the shared representation being operated on.**" *(Notes from the Lab, May 2025)*

**And the hole he leaves open in his own thesis:**

> "While free addressability focuses on making system parts targetable, **it doesn't address how those parts can actually be modified.** The DOM is exploitable because CSS selectors provide addressing while referenced elements expose clear manipulation interfaces — **a relationship that remains undertheorized in our work.**"

**Canvases as substrate.** Mastodon, Causal Islands LA thread, 2024-03-30:

> "**canvases fit all the criteria of an information substrate**, and I think looking at them this way opens up their 'true' design space. **Not just tools or whiteboards, but a digital medium with a consistent grammar and semantics.**"

> "An '**integration substrate**' would be an information substrate which is **suitable for the integration of other substrates, interfaces, and interactions. A shared medium in which many disparate interfaces can coexist and be combined.**"

> "**Canvases could be to HCI what Haskell is to Type Systems research.**" *(TfT Rocks 2024 deck)*

**DOM ↔ Canvas**, 2024-03-25:

> "**What if *any* website could be turned into a canvas with a single click?** … **The regular web, zero magic.**"

> "**A canvas can have just as much layout control as the DOM, if we let it. With a little work, they could be indistinguishable.**"

**Breadboard** — Ink & Switch lab note, 2026-03-20, the most current and the most useful to us:

> "Patchwork is built from aspirationally composable parts… but **the composition itself is hidden.** … **The assembly disappears behind a finished-looking application.**"

> "Each `<patchwork-view>` **carries its wiring as HTML attributes: `tool-id` and `doc-url`.** … The mechanism is simple: `document.querySelectorAll('patchwork-view')`. **Breadboard doesn't ask the frame for cooperation. It finds views the same way a browser extension or screen reader would — by reading what's already public in the DOM.**"

> "**This didn't require building an inspection API. I merely stopped hiding what was already there.** Both experiments **treat the DOM not as a rendering target but as a substrate with useful properties: public attributes, sibling order, containment.**"

> "**Breadboard is postmodernist: it works with what exists**… **Space-frame is modernist: it replaces what exists**… **Neither approach is obviously correct.**"

> "**Frameworks treat the DOM as a rendering target.** Build systems assume a uni-directional flow from source code to deployed artifact. The resulting software is not meant to be modified by its users. **React's virtual DOM, for example, can make DOM-level changes fragile as it assumes unilateral control over the DOM subtree it manages.**"

> "**Digital systems should aspire to the properties of visibility and editability of physical breadboards, but not the material constraints.** … **Malleability does not imply fragility.**"

**On browser primitives — his most recent relevant statement, 2026-05-02:**

> "The nascent **HTML-in-Canvas API** is exciting to me **not because of flashy effects, but because it extends the semantics that the DOM can (tractably) represent** — as a tiny example, it's possible to **show an element in multiple places, cheaply, under arbitrary transforms.**"

`!` **Read that last one twice. "Show an element in multiple places, cheaply, under arbitrary transforms" is transclusion, stated as a browser primitive.** It is the same operation as OCIF §Nodes-as-Resources, and the same one DreamDOM has to provide.

**On OCIF specifically**, he is by far its quietest founder — zero issues, two discussions, three comments, four PRs across the whole org; **never mentioned OCIF on Bluesky at all**; not mentioned in his own Canvas Research Memo. His substantive contribution is in **Meeting 1**, and it is the argument that shaped the extension mechanism we have been analysing:

> "when we look at something like **email**, part of what allowed it to evolve was… **a very very tiny set of required information**… Email does actually allow for **arbitrary other fields** to be added… then the other one is something like **XML** which has support for **explicit schemas and also ways to compose them together**. So we could imagine… **some base versioned spec that has a very minimal set of absolutely mandatory stuff**, but then on top of that… **more kind of opt-in specs**… that allows **more kind of localized coordination** and still gives you that interop where it applies, **without bringing all of that into a spec that just tries to do everything.**"

**Meeting 5**, on graceful degradation across schemas:

> "**another tool that didn't have that would still be able to treat it as a rectangle if they shared some base schema, even if they wouldn't understand how to wire up edges to ports.**"

**Meeting 3** — the fallback he proposed, which was never built, and which is **exactly the mitigation for the data loss I measured in §3**:

> "I really like the idea of having as a suggested fallback mechanism… that kind of **per object text view** — so in the cases where you don't have schema support for something, being able to see like '**I don't actually know how to render this but I can still show you the text of just this object**'… you have that kind of **escape hatch mechanism.**"

`!` Two and a half years later, the implementation that drops Berlin is failing at precisely the point the founder said needed an escape hatch.

And from an LLM-generated summary of Meeting 39 — **flagged as paraphrase, not his words:**

> "Orion emphasized: Desire to see [OCIF] implemented in real tools… **Has some influence and can likely get at least one major implementation effort, but can probably 'pull that favor' only once.** Therefore, wants to **stabilize key semantics like rotation and transforms before pushing for adoption.**"

His current position, `orionreed.com/posts/app-file-topology/`, 2026-02-26 — the one most likely to matter to a sovereign-vessel project:

> "**Enclosure did not need to abolish the file. It needed only to make the boundary opaque.**"

> "even robust export — Google Takeout, Notion's markdown output, platform APIs — **rarely preserves the full relational structure of work produced within a platform**… **The topology has changed such that the file is no longer a sufficient vessel for the work.**"

---

# 9. The closing three

## CAN OCIF CARRY A TIDDLER?

**Conditionally yes — and every condition is on us, not on the format.**

The format itself is sufficient. §Extension Mechanism grants a URI-identified property bag in which "the extension can use all other property keys" and "**arbitrary, nested JSON structures are allowed**," usable on nodes, resources, representations *and* the canvas, and §Defining Extensions plus the cookbook confirm it may be minted and used "in the same OCIF document, **without any prior announcement or registration process**." I built `@lar/tiddler` carrying our lar URI, TiddlyWiki fields including `$:/`-prefixed system tags, and wikilink/transclusion/macro syntax in a `text/vnd.tiddlywiki` representation, and it validated against the official `spec/v0.7.0/schema.json` with **zero errors** and round-tripped **byte-identical** through the live implementation. Typed relations have a real home: §Edge Extension's `rel` "allows representing an **RDF triple (subject, predicate, object) as (start, rel, end)**," and `@ocif/hyperedge` generalises it to ordered, directed, weighted n-ary relations. Our renderer stays ours: §Resources is MIME-typed with an **ordered fallback chain** where "the first representation is the default… later representations can be used as fallbacks," so `text/vnd.tiddlywiki` first with `text/html` and `text/plain` behind it is the pattern the spec intends. Transclusion is named in our sense — §Nodes as Resources: "Using a node B as a resource in another node A can be seen as a form of **transclusion**… the intention is to create a **live view (not a static image)**… Whenever the way B looks is changed, the other places where node B is imported should be updated" — with cycle refusal ("a node MAY NOT directly or indirectly import itself… all stated imports of the loop MUST be ignored"). And §Nesting Canvases lets an entire sub-lararium be a node, with a specified RFC 7396 merge and an explicit exception preserving extension arrays.

**Three conditions bind, and they are the whole answer.** *(a)* **Preservation is not normatively required.** `grep` for RFC 2119 over the spec returns nothing; there is no conformance section and no notion of a conforming consumer; the only MUST touching the case says "unknown extensions **MUST be ignored**" (§Canvas Extensions), while preservation appears as a lowercase "apps **should** preserve unknown extensions and round-trip them on export" (§Data Extension) and as keyword-free narrative (§Handling Extension Data), with the strongest wording — "an app **may not** alter or delete data written be another app" — living in `design/requirements.md`, which the spec does not incorporate. The byte-identical survival I measured is therefore a property of one implementation's good faith, **not a guarantee of the format**; any other tool may legally drop us and remain exactly as conformant. *(b)* **In the field today, in the only maintained implementation, any node bearing a text resource is silently deleted** — the spec's own Berlin/Germany Hello World comes back missing Berlin, both text resources, and the `rel` predicate. I have ruled that an implementation bug (§3h) rather than a spec requirement or ambiguity, on the strength of the spec explicitly prescribing the placeholder rectangle and the representation fallback chain, both of which the reader bypasses — which means it is a filed bug and a small PR, not a dead end. *(c)* **OCIF carries the *fact* of transclusion, not its *position*.** `{{title}}` inside a paragraph has no OCIF representation; it must live inside our extension's opaque payload. A foreign tool can move, group, connect and nest our node; it can never edit what is inside it.

**So, stated plainly: OCIF can carry a tiddler as a faithfully-preserved opaque payload attached to a node whose geometry, hierarchy, grouping and typed relations foreign tools can genuinely manipulate — and it cannot carry a tiddler as something a foreign tool can meaningfully read.** That is interop at the **arrangement** layer, not the **content** layer. Which is, in fairness, exactly what OCIF says it is. kepano asked this precise question in Discussion #44 eighteen months ago, and the chair answered: "**the OCWG and the OCIF format can really only enable _interchange_. True interoperability will have to be a choice by each tool-maker.**"

## WHAT PULLING THIS THREAD ACTUALLY COSTS US

**Adapter work — small, and mostly work our own model implies anyway.**

- **The extension.** One document plus a JSON Schema at a URI we host and actually serve as JSON (`bags/…` → resolvable https). Half a day. `!` **Mint our own URI. Never ride `@ocif/data`** — its same-`type` RFC 7386 merge rule makes it a shared mutable global across applications, and its schema pins `type` to a constant so collision is guaranteed, not incidental.
- **The codec over the Automerge composite store.** Our node/parent/local-transform model must align with OCIF's TRS chain (`M = T*R*S`, composed root-to-leaf); `@ocif/global-positions` gives us a *declared cache slot* for what DreamDOM computes anyway, with the conflict rule already specified in our favour ("the local position MUST be preferred"). Estimate **1–2 weeks for a faithful writer, 2–3 for a reader that survives hostile input.**
- **The fallback chain is non-optional armour.** `text/vnd.tiddlywiki` first, `text/html` and `text/plain` behind. It will not save us from `tldraw/ocif` today (which drops the node before consulting it) but it will save us from every SVG renderer, every indexer, and every future reader — and it is Orion's own unbuilt "per object text view" escape hatch, which gives us a principled name for it in any OCWG conversation.
- **ID discipline, welded before it ships.** §ID wants IDs to be valid CSS identifiers "if possible"; `lar:///…` is not one; and every implementation I ran rewrites ids regardless (`n1` → `shape:n1`). `!` **Carry the lar URI inside the extension; use a stable opaque id outside.** An id rewrite through one foreign tool dangles every `@ocif/edge` `start`/`end`, every `@ocif/group.members` entry, and every `@ocif/ports` reference in the file. *(Standing practice: weld-test the naming before the rename, not after.)*
- **A round-trip witness in our own suite** — `tools/ocif-roundtrip-witness.sh` — pushing a real tiddler corpus through our writer, our reader, and `tldraw/ocif` (MIT, adaptable, defects already located), asserting on the **decoded payload**, not the bytes. Note their 91 green tests: a fixture that omits the failing case proves nothing.

**Governance participation — cheap, and the seat is empty.** Bi-weekly Tuesdays, Discord, GitHub Discussions, no charter, no fee, no sponsor, four humans have ever committed to the spec repo. **What we would owe is exactly what the CR asks for: implementation experience.** Two contributions are ours to make and would land immediately: **(i)** the missing normative layer — an RFC 2119 clause, a conformance section defining a conforming consumer, and a preserve-and-passthrough MUST, which is the single change that would make OCIF worth *standing on* rather than merely *exporting to*; **(ii)** the round-trip test bench they have wanted since March 2024 and never built — and I already have four failing cases against the spec's own Hello World, which is a contribution rather than a complaint. Realistic cost: **1–2 hours a fortnight plus the artefacts.**

`!` **The real risks, named.** *The group may not be meeting* — every public surface is silent since April, so I would open a Discussion or a Discord message *before* writing a line of spec text, and treat a non-answer as the answer. *There is no IPR policy* — contributing spec text to a body with no patent or copyright policy, under CC BY-SA, is a boundary question, not a technical one, and it belongs to the operator. *Filing `@lar/tiddler` publicly declares part of our data model in a CC BY-SA venue* — same call, same owner. *The format may move under us* — v0.7.0 merged nodes and relations and flipped coordinates from global to local in one release; a v0.8 could do the same again, and our codec would follow.

## WHAT IT BUYS

**`*` A seat in a room where our nakama already are.** Orion Reed founded OCWG. **chee is Patchwork's top contributor and already ships an Automerge-backed OCIF document rendered through two pluggable canvas views** (`chee/Littlebook`) — our exact architecture, prototyped, at OCIF v0.4, unattended since August 2025. Ink & Switch's GAIOS ships `tldraw4` and `tldraw5` as Patchwork tool modules. This is not a cold approach to strangers; it is a thread already tied at the other end.

**`_` A settled, published vocabulary for the arrangement layer** that we would otherwise design from scratch and get wrong once: local transforms with a declared global cache and a stated conflict rule; ports-as-nodes; ordered directed weighted hyperedges; property inheritance with include/exclude and cycle refusal; theme-as-possible-worlds over arbitrary extension data; and a nesting-canvases rule with an explicit merge-patch semantics and an explicit extension-array exception. **Even if we never exchange a single file with a foreign tool, reading this specification is cheaper than re-deriving it** — and §Nesting Canvases plus §Nodes-as-Resources is a better-specified transclusion boundary than we currently have written down anywhere.

**`*` A 2,279-line MIT reference implementation** of that vocabulary (`tldraw/ocif` `src/ocif.ts` plus 2,598 lines of tests), legible and adaptable, whose extension-preservation machinery is exactly the pattern we want and whose two defects I have already located and can cite.

**`!` An exit path that is a mechanism rather than a promise.** Per Orion's own 2026 essay, "the file is no longer a sufficient vessel for the work" because export "rarely preserves the full relational structure." OCIF is the one live attempt to make a canvas file *be* that vessel. It does not yet succeed — but a sovereign vessel that can hand its user a file another tool can genuinely open is making a different claim about ownership than one that cannot.

**`0` And the honest reading, which I would rather state than dress up: OCIF is an export format today and could become an interop surface if someone supplies the conformance language and the test bench.** Nobody is currently doing that. A v0.7.1 draft has sat unreleased since April. **That is a vacancy with our name on it**, and it is the cheapest possible way to find out whether anyone is still home.

**My recommendation, plainly:** build `@lar/tiddler` and the codec now — the cost is small and the format is sufficient for the arrangement layer. Treat OCIF as **export-plus-preserve**, never as a store; the Automerge doc stays sovereign. **Build no feature whose correctness depends on a foreign tool preserving our data**, because I measured that it does not. File the Berlin bug against `tldraw/ocif` with the isolation table, and open one Discussion in `orgs/ocwg` with the four failing Hello-World cases — that single act is simultaneously the cheapest governance contribution available to us, a real gift to the two people still doing the work, and the fastest way to learn whether the group is alive.

---

**Confidence: 16/20.** Solid on the spec text, the tooling state, and the round-trip measurements — those I read, ran, and controlled. Weaker on whether OCWG is dormant or merely quiet (the editor's own Java implementation was active five weeks ago, which cuts against my stall reading), and on Orion's current appetite for OCIF, where the only evidence of intent is an LLM-generated paraphrase of a meeting summary.

**Owed / not measured, so you know the shape of the hole:** the **OCWG Discord** is auth-walled and I could not read it — the meeting notes reference substantive spec discussion happening there that is invisible to this entire report, and it is the most likely place for evidence that would move my stall ruling in either direction. Also unmeasured: whether `tldraw/ocif`'s text-resource drop is known-and-backlogged or simply unnoticed; whether `chee/Littlebook`'s OCIF plugin is abandoned or paused; and whether the `v0.7.1-draft` has a release blocker or merely lacks a hand on the tiller.
